{"title":"Transformations","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><p>A transformation is a mapping of a (sometimes projective) plane to itself so that each point has a unique image and also has a unique point that was mapped to it. They include <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Translation\" title=\"Translation\">Translations</a>, <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Reflection\" title=\"Reflection\">Reflections</a>, <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Homothety\" title=\"Homothety\">Dilations/Homethecy</a>, and <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Circular_Inversion\" title=\"Circular Inversion\">Inversion</a>.\n</p><p>More generally, however, a transformation can also denote a mapping from one arbitary <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Metric_space\" title=\"Metric space\">metric space</a> to another. \n</p><p><i>This article is a stub.  Help us out by <span class=\"plainlinks\"><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/index.php?title=Transformations&amp;action=edit\">expanding it</a></span>.</i>\n</p>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261018112313\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: []\nCPU time usage: 0.009 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.017 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 7/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 286/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 0/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 4/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 0/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%    6.621      1 Template:Stub\n100.00%    6.621      1 -total\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wiki-wiki_:pcache:14434:|#|:idhash:canonical and timestamp 20261018112313 and revision id 239623. Rendering was triggered because: api-parse\n -->\n</div>"}